/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A steam table lists measured properties of water and steam, because no single simple equation covers compressed liquid, wet steam and superheated steam.
   - Tables use absolute pressure.
   - pabs = pgauge + patm
   - pabs is absolute pressure, pgauge the gauge reading, patm the local atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar at sea level); all three in the same unit, bar or kPa.
   - The saturated table lists pressure with Tsat or temperature with psat.
   - Inside the dome, pressure and temperature are not independent, so a table row alone cannot fix the state.
   - Above Tsat the steam is superheated and the saturated table stops helping.
   - Printed tables jump in steps, often 50 K, so a given temperature may fall between two columns.
   - y is the property wanted (h, v or s); (T₁, y₁) and (T₂, y₂) are the two table entries either side; T is the given temperature in °C.
   - The Mollier chart, also called the h-s diagram, plots enthalpy h in kJ/kg upward and entropy s in kJ/kg·K across.
*/
