/* book Worked example: Q: Steam flows at 10 bar absolute and 280 °C. (a) State whether it is superheated, saturated or compressed liquid. (b) The superheated table for 10 bar gives h = 2943.1 kJ/kg, v = 0.2327 m³/kg, s = 6.9265 kJ/kg·K at 250 °C, and h = 3051.6 kJ/kg, v = 0.2580 m³/kg, s = 7.1246 kJ/kg·K at 300 °C. Find h, v and s at 280 °C by interpolation.
   1. At 10 bar, Tsat = 179.9 °C. Given T = 280 °C > 179.9 °C, so the steam is superheated by 100.1 K; the saturated table cannot give h, v or s.
   2. Fraction of the interval: f = (280 − 250)/(300 − 250) = 30/50 = 0.6.
   3. h = 2943.1 + 0.6 × (3051.6 − 2943.1) = 2943.1 + 0.6 × 108.5 = 2943.1 + 65.1 = 3008.2 kJ/kg.
   4. v = 0.2327 + 0.6 × (0.2580 − 0.2327) = 0.2327 + 0.6 × 0.0253 = 0.2327 + 0.0152 = 0.2479 m³/kg.
   5. s = 6.9265 + 0.6 × (7.1246 − 6.9265) = 6.9265 + 0.6 × 0.1981 = 6.9265 + 0.1189 = 7.0454 kJ/kg·K.
   6. Check: hg at 10 bar is 2777.1 kJ/kg; 3008.2 is larger, as superheated steam must be. Tables with a 280 °C entry give 3008.6 kJ/kg, so linear interpolation is off by only 0.4 kJ/kg.
   Answer: Superheated steam: h ≈ 3008.2 kJ/kg, v ≈ 0.2479 m³/kg, s ≈ 7.0454 kJ/kg·K. */
