/* book Worked example 2: Q: Steam at 10 bar and 250 °C (h₁ = 2943.1 kJ/kg, s₁ = 6.9265 kJ/kg·K) expands isentropically in a turbine to 1 bar. At 1 bar: hf = 417.5 kJ/kg, hfg = 2257.4 kJ/kg, sf = 1.3028 kJ/kg·K, sfg = 6.0561 kJ/kg·K, sg = 7.3589 kJ/kg·K. Find the dryness fraction and enthalpy after expansion, and the drop in enthalpy.
   1. Isentropic means s₂ = s₁ = 6.9265 kJ/kg·K. On the Mollier chart this is a vertical line down from the starting point.
   2. Compare with sg at 1 bar: 6.9265 < 7.3589, so the end state lies inside the dome: wet steam.
   3. Dryness fraction: x₂ = (s₂ − sf)/sfg = (6.9265 − 1.3028)/6.0561 = 5.6237/6.0561 = 0.9286.
   4. Enthalpy: h₂ = hf + x₂ hfg = 417.5 + 0.9286 × 2257.4 = 417.5 + 2096.2 = 2513.7 kJ/kg.
   5. Enthalpy drop: h₁ − h₂ = 2943.1 − 2513.7 = 429.4 kJ/kg. This is the ideal work per kg of steam in the turbine.
   6. Chart check: the vertical line from (s = 6.93, h = 2943) meets the 1 bar line just above the x = 0.92 curve, near h = 2514 kJ/kg, which agrees.
   Answer: x₂ = 0.929, h₂ = 2513.7 kJ/kg, and the isentropic enthalpy drop is 429.4 kJ/kg. */
