/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Wet steam at p₁ enters a throttle, a small orifice or valve.
   - hf1 is saturated liquid enthalpy at inlet pressure p₁, kJ/kg; hfg1 is latent heat at p₁, kJ/kg; x₁ is inlet dryness fraction.
   - To use this, the outlet must be superheated.
   - If the outlet is still wet, h₂ depends on an unknown outlet dryness x₂.
   - A separating calorimeter works mechanically.
   - This instrument suits very wet steam, but it is less exact: some fine droplets pass with the steam, so xs overstates the dryness.
*/
