/* book Worked example: Q: Wet steam at 10 bar is throttled to 1 bar and 130 °C. At 10 bar, hf = 762.5 kJ/kg and hfg = 2014.6 kJ/kg. At 1 bar and 130 °C, h = 2736.7 kJ/kg. Find the inlet dryness fraction x₁.
   1. Check the outlet state: at 1 bar the saturation temperature is 99.6 °C, so 130 °C is superheated by about 30 K.
   2. Read the outlet enthalpy from the superheated table: h₂ = 2736.7 kJ/kg.
   3. Throttling: h₁ = h₂ = 2736.7 kJ/kg.
   4. At the inlet pressure 10 bar: hf1 = 762.5 kJ/kg and hfg1 = 2014.6 kJ/kg.
   5. x₁ = (2736.7 − 762.5)/2014.6 = 1974.2/2014.6 = 0.980.
   6. Check: 0.980 is above the limit 0.949 found for 10 bar, so the outlet can indeed be superheated.
   Answer: x₁ ≈ 0.980, so the steam is 98.0 per cent dry. */
